97
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:42,900
Most people don't realize it, but there

98
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,100
is an exact definition of a horsepower, which

99
00:05:45,101 --> 00:05:48,440
which is 33,000 foot-pounds per minute.

100
00:05:48,441 --> 00:05:51,440
If you didn't quite get that, don't worry.

101
00:05:51,441 --> 00:05:56,240
Just remember that horsepower is work done over time.

102
00:05:56,241 --> 00:05:57,740
I've got about 100 of you under my hood.

103
00:05:57,741 --> 00:05:59,740
Yeah.

104
00:05:59,741 --> 00:06:03,360
But you may wonder where the horse comes in.

105
00:06:03,361 --> 00:06:07,320
In the late 1700s, James Watt was the struggling inventor

106
00:06:07,321 --> 00:06:13,660
of a new type of steam engine and also a savvy salesman.

107
00:06:13,661 --> 00:06:15,100
And since his customers were basically

108
00:06:15,101 --> 00:06:16,340
to do their work.

109
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:18,440
He needed to be able to tell his customers, look,

110
00:06:18,540 --> 00:06:19,840
I have a steam engine here that'll

111
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:22,440
replace two of your horses or four of your horses.

112
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:24,540
And in order to do that, he had to actually go out

113
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:26,540
and watch a horse.

114
00:06:26,640 --> 00:06:29,140
There are a few theories about how James Watt created

115
00:06:29,240 --> 00:06:31,860
the concept of horsepower.

116
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:34,160
According to the most popular one,

117
00:06:34,260 --> 00:06:37,860
he observed a horse on a grinding mill wheel.

118
00:06:37,960 --> 00:06:41,100
It was something like a horse on a device we know today,

119
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:45,100
a hot walker, used to cool down horses after a walk.

120
00:06:45,101 --> 00:06:49,100
However, the horse Watt saw met resistance when connected to the

121
00:06:49,101 --> 00:06:50,100
grinding wheel.

122
00:06:50,101 --> 00:06:56,100
Watt calculated that the horse pulled with a force of 180 pounds.

123
00:06:56,101 --> 00:07:00,100
He multiplied that by the horse's speed, which he determined to be

124
00:07:00,101 --> 00:07:03,100
181 feet per minute.

125
00:07:03,101 --> 00:07:07,100
After rounding up the number, Watt arrived at nearly 33,000

126
00:07:07,101 --> 00:07:10,100
pounds, lifted one foot per minute.

127
00:07:10,101 --> 00:07:15,700
minute. And voila, he defined horsepower

128
00:07:15,701 --> 00:07:19,820
as work done over time. How does this translate into

129
00:07:19,821 --> 00:07:26,180
horsepower for a car engine? Here's how it works.

130
00:07:26,181 --> 00:07:29,260
An internal combustion engine ignites a combination of fuel

131
00:07:29,261 --> 00:07:33,700
and air. The explosion drives a piston

132
00:07:33,701 --> 00:07:36,620
downward. The piston's movement turns a

133
00:07:36,621 --> 00:07:40,520
crankshaft. The amount of turning force on

134
00:07:40,521 --> 00:07:46,160
the crankshaft is known as torque. An example of torque is

135
00:07:46,161 --> 00:07:48,960
when a wrench is used to loosen a nut.

136
00:07:48,961 --> 00:07:52,760
The more force or torque applied to the nut, the easier it is to

137
00:07:52,761 --> 00:07:58,180
unfasten. So horsepower is the turning

138
00:07:58,181 --> 00:08:02,420
force of torque, measured in foot pounds, combined with a number of

139
00:08:02,421 --> 00:08:07,720
rotations per minute. This is work done over time.

140
00:08:07,721 --> 00:08:12,160
And again, one engine generated horsepower equals 33,000 foot

141
00:08:12,161 --> 00:08:16,960
pounds per minute. How do humans stack up in

142
00:08:16,961 --> 00:08:24,080
horsepower generation? The average person can sustain

143
00:08:24,081 --> 00:08:29,580
only about 1 10th horsepower when riding a bicycle.

144
00:08:29,581 --> 00:08:32,580
Of course, a world-class biker like Lance Armstrong

145
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:35,180
would do better, but he'd only maintain

146
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:36,680
about one-quarter horsepower.

147
00:08:40,220 --> 00:08:42,720
But no matter how strong a rider is,

148
00:08:42,820 --> 00:08:46,740
there's only so much a human can do.

149
00:08:46,840 --> 00:08:50,740
We get tired, we go slower, and eventually come to a stop.

150
00:08:52,940 --> 00:08:54,240
We just don't have the stamina.

151
00:08:54,340 --> 00:08:57,240
We don't have the endurance of a mechanical machine.

152
00:08:57,241 --> 00:08:59,880
Mechanical horsepower became a key selling point

153
00:08:59,980 --> 00:09:03,040
for many car makers, from the early 1900s

154
00:09:03,140 --> 00:09:09,980
with the Ford Model T at 10 horsepower,

155
00:09:10,080 --> 00:09:15,000
to the muscle cars of the 50s, like the 283 horsepower

156
00:09:15,100 --> 00:09:22,900
Corvette, to today's Saleen S7 at a monstrous 750 horsepower.

157
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:24,900
The car industry has sold customers

158
00:09:24,901 --> 00:09:26,900
on ever-increasing amounts of horsepower.

159
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:32,900
And yet, there was a problem.

160
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,540
From the beginning, manufacturers measured

161
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:37,840
